## Server Setup

### Overview

Server setup is the process of preparing a server environment to host and run an application. It includes installing the required operating system packages, configuring the web server, setting up the application, connecting the database, and applying basic security settings.

### Server Setup Responsibilities

* Install and configure the server operating system.
* Install required software and dependencies.
* Configure the web server such as Apache or Nginx.
* Deploy the application files to the server.
* Configure the database connection.
* Set environment variables and application settings.
* Configure firewall and required network ports.
* Enable SSL/HTTPS for secure communication.
* Start and monitor application services.
* Create backups and maintain server logs.

### Example: Ubuntu Server Setup

```bash
# Update the server
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y

# Install required packages
sudo apt install nginx git curl -y

# Check Nginx status
sudo systemctl status nginx

# Enable Nginx at system startup
sudo systemctl enable nginx

# Start Nginx
sudo systemctl start nginx

# Clone the application
cd /var/www/
sudo git clone https://github.com/example/my-application.git

# Set application permissions
sudo chown -R www-data:www-data /var/www/my-application
sudo chmod -R 755 /var/www/my-application
```

### Nginx Configuration

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;

    root /var/www/my-application;
    index index.html index.htm;

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
    }
}
```

After creating the configuration:

```bash
sudo nginx -t
sudo systemctl reload nginx
```

### Firewall Configuration

```bash
sudo ufw allow OpenSSH
sudo ufw allow 'Nginx Full'
sudo ufw enable
sudo ufw status
```

### Application Deployment

```bash
cd /var/www/my-application

# Install dependencies
npm install

# Build the application
npm run build

# Start the application
npm start
```

### Verification

```bash
# Check web server
sudo systemctl status nginx

# Check open ports
sudo ss -tulpn

# Test the application
curl http://localhost
```

The server is considered ready when the application is accessible, required services are running, the database connection is working, and the necessary security configurations are enabled.
